<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Raúl Alfonsín</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1927">1927</a>–<a href="/years/2009">2009</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Raúl Alfonsín was an Argentine lawyer and Radical Civic Union politician who served as president from 1983 to 1989, the first elected after the military <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dictatorship">dictatorship</a>. He set up a commission to investigate the disappeared and allowed the trial of the juntas in 1985. Hyperinflation and military unrest marked his later years, and he handed over power early to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carlos-menem">Carlos Menem</a> in 1989.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/raul-alfonsin.jpg" alt="Raúl Alfonsín" width="320" height="426" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Presidencia de la Nación Argentina · CC BY 2.5 ar · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ra%C3%BAl_Alfons%C3%ADn_con_banda_presidencial_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Raúl Ricardo Alfonsín was born on 12 March 1927 in Chascomús, 123 kilometres south of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buenos-aires">Buenos Aires</a>. His parents were shopkeepers. He attended the General San Martín Military Lyceum, because his father thought a military school would match a private school in quality at less cost. Alfonsín disliked the military, but the schooling helped him understand its outlook. He did not pursue a military career and studied law at the National University of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/la-plata">La Plata</a> and the University of Buenos Aires.</p><p>He joined the Radical Civic Union, in the faction of Ricardo Balbín, and was elected to the legislature of Buenos Aires Province in 1958. During the National Reorganization Process he opposed both sides of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dirty-war">Dirty War</a> and filed writs of habeas corpus on behalf of victims of forced disappearance. After Balbín's death he became leader of the party and won the 1983 election.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">In office</h2><p>Alfonsín governed from 10 December 1983 as the first democratically elected president after the military regime. Three days after taking office he sent Congress a bill to revoke the military's self-amnesty law and ordered cases against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guerrilla-warfare">guerrilla</a> leaders and the heads of the juntas. He set up the National Commission on the Disappearance of Persons, whose Nunca más report documented the scope of the crimes, and the Trial of the Juntas followed. Discontent in the army led to the Carapintada mutinies, which he answered with the full stop law and the law of Due Obedience.</p><p>His government settled the Beagle dispute, increased trade with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brazil">Brazil</a> and legalised divorce in 1987, over the opposition of the Church. It clashed with the trade unions, which <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peronism">Peronism</a> still controlled. The UCR lost its legislative majority in 1987, and the Austral plan and the Spring plan failed to bring <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/inflation">inflation</a> under control. After the Peronist Carlos Menem won the 1989 election, Alfonsín handed over power on 8 July, five months earlier than scheduled.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Alfonsín led the UCR again in later years and negotiated the Pact of Olivos with Menem, which set the terms for the constitutional amendment of 1994. Historians disagree about it: Félix Luna thought it a necessary evil, while Miguel Angel de Marco and Fernando Rocchi saw it as the biggest mistake of his career. He died of lung cancer on 31 March 2009, aged 82, and was buried in La Recoleta Cemetery.</p><p>The same historians praise his role in the aftermath of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dirty-war">Dirty War</a> and the restoration of democracy. He received the Princess of Asturias Award for international cooperation in 1985.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Raúl Alfonsín” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Raúl Alfonsín</b> of the Radical Civic Union took office in December 1983, a commission chaired by the writer Ernesto Sabato documented the disappearances in the report Nunca más, and the junta leaders were tried in 1985, although later laws and pardons halted further prosecutions until they were annulled…</blockquote><a href="/empire/argentine-republic">Argentine Republic · Into the Present</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Chascomús, in what is now <a href="/countries/argentina">Argentina</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buenos-aires">Buenos Aires</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/argentina">Argentina</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=-35.58,-58.01&amp;year=1980" aria-label="Chascomús on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="3084 3449 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Raúl Alfonsín"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M3534 3719h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M3534 3719h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=-35.58,-58.01&amp;year=1980">What was here in 1980? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
